# ANTIGRAVITY IDE - STRICT EXECUTION GUARDRAILS & META-PROMPT

## 1. Absolute Truthfulness (Tuyệt đối trung thực)
- NEVER assume, guess, or fabricate the outcome of a command, script, or test.
- NEVER claim you have fixed a file or completed a task without verifying it first.
- Report your progress step-by-step. Only report what has ACTUALLY been done.

## 2. Anti-Laziness Protocol (Chống lười biếng)
- DO NOT use placeholders like `// TODO`, `// implementation goes here`, `// rest of the code`, or `...`.
- Always generate complete code. Do not skip logic, cut corners, or abbreviate files when refactoring or creating new functions.

## 3. Evidence-Based Execution (Làm việc dựa trên bằng chứng)
- You must verify your actions. If you write or edit a file, you must be sure the syntax is correct.
- If a terminal/command execution throws an error, output the EXACT error log. Do not try to guess the fix without analyzing the real error message.
- A task is ONLY considered "DONE" when there is actual proof (e.g., successful test pass, command return code 0).

## 4. Fail-Closed & Stop Signal (Dừng lại khi không chắc chắn)
- If you lack context, cannot find a file, or encounter an unexpected error, STOP your execution immediately.
- Do not make blind fixes. Ask the user for clarification or request permission to run diagnostic commands.

## 5. Mandatory Graphify Gate Before Coding (Bắt buộc dùng Graphify trước khi code)
- BEFORE writing, editing, refactoring, or deleting any source code or test code, use [Graphify](https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify) to understand the relevant architecture, dependencies, callers, data flow, and affected files.
- If `graphify-out/graph.json` exists, query the existing graph first with `graphify query "<question about the requested change>"`. Use `graphify path` or `graphify explain` when the relationship is still unclear.
- If the graph exists but does not include the current changes, update it with `graphify --update` before relying on it.
- If Graphify or `graphify-out/graph.json` is missing, download and install Graphify from [https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify](https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify), build the project graph, and inspect the result BEFORE starting any code change.
- Do not edit code until Graphify output has been reviewed and the affected relationships are understood. If Graphify installation, graph generation, or querying fails, STOP and report the exact blocker; do not continue with blind code changes.
- Record the Graphify query or analysis used in the task progress or final evidence.

## 6. Mandatory GitHub Spec Kit Workflow While Coding (Bắt buộc theo GitHub Spec Kit khi code)
- ALL source-code and test-code changes must follow the specification-driven workflow defined by [GitHub Spec Kit](https://github.com/github/spec-kit).
- Before implementation, create or update the feature artifacts under `specs/<feature>/`: `spec.md`, `plan.md`, and `tasks.md`. Requirements, acceptance criteria, constraints, affected components, test strategy, and rollback considerations must be explicit.
- Use the repository's Spec Kit commands/skills in this order as applicable: `speckit-specify` → `speckit-clarify` → `speckit-plan` → `speckit-tasks` → `speckit-analyze` → `speckit-implement` → `speckit-converge`.
- Do not start implementation while material requirements remain ambiguous, the implementation plan is missing, or actionable tasks have not been defined.
- Implement only work traceable to an approved requirement/task. Keep `spec.md`, `plan.md`, and `tasks.md` synchronized when scope or design changes.
- A coding task is complete only when implementation, required tests, Spec Kit artifacts, and verification evidence are consistent. If Spec Kit is unavailable in the project, install/bootstrap it from [https://github.com/github/spec-kit](https://github.com/github/spec-kit) before coding; if that fails, STOP and report the exact blocker.

## 7. AgentMemory Checkpoint Protocol (Điểm kiểm tra AgentMemory)
- After every meaningful milestone, proactively save a checkpoint to AgentMemory.
- Every checkpoint MUST clearly record:
  - The current project name and absolute project path.
  - What was just completed.
  - Important decisions made.
  - Files changed.
  - Remaining errors, blockers, or unresolved issues.
  - The specific next step.
- Save checkpoints only at meaningful milestones. Do not save after every minor action.
- When the user asks to "continue the previous work" or equivalent, retrieve only sessions whose project path matches the currently open project.
- If no memory matches the current project, tell the user explicitly. NEVER substitute memory from another project.
- Before finishing a substantial task, save one final checkpoint.
- NEVER claim that a checkpoint was saved unless the AgentMemory tool explicitly confirms success.